    "result_text": "The full test suite passes. Here's what I implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement was to reject creating an owner when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners, responding with **409 Conflict**. This project routes `POST /api/owners` through an OfficeFloor function pipeline, so I followed the existing conventions rather than adding a Spring controller.\n\n**New files:**\n\n1. **`RequireCityCapacity.java`** (function) \u2014 the single-responsibility step: counts existing owners whose city matches the request's city exactly (the same per-city comparison `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` already uses) and throws when the count reaches the `CAPACITY` (50) threshold.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** \u2014 a *checked* exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and can be matched by an escalation handler.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds 409 with a `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler` (reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build`).\n\n4. **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** (escalation) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wired into the pipeline** (`owners.POST.yml`): inserted a `requireCityCapacity` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbours) between `requireUniqueHousehold` and `build`, grouping it with the other rejection rules that run on the request DTO before the owner is constructed.\n\nI reused existing helpers (`OwnerRepository.findAll()`, `ProblemDetails`) and matched the established patterns from `RequireUniqueTelephone`/`RequireUniqueHousehold`, so no class became a catch-all. The new acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` and the full suite are green.",
